<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Northumberland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Northumberland is the northernmost county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, between the Tyne and the Tweed, crossed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>. Formerly the northern part of the Anglian kingdom of Northumbria, it was long disputed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> I's son Henry held the earldom from 1139, until Henry II reclaimed it in 1157. Its border lands remained a zone of raids and fortified towers into the sixteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/northumberland.jpg" alt="Northumberland" width="320" height="158" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: mattbuck · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bamburgh_MMB_55_Bamburgh_Castle_(edited,_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
